// GitHub issue #254 design conclusion (2026-08-09): ASID assignment must be
// independent of a page-table storage slot. A heap-owned page table has no
// slot from which to derive an ASID; this allocator gives it a matching
// lifetime.
//
// GitHub issue #402 removed the last fixed ceiling on process creation from
// this file, in the two halves that issue named.
//
// **The width is asked for, not assumed.** ID_AA64MMFR0_EL1.ASIDBits says
// whether the implementation has 8-bit or 16-bit ASIDs; both QEMU virt
// (Cortex-A53) and RPi5 (Cortex-A76) report 16, which is 65535 assignable
// numbers rather than the 255 the architectural floor guarantees.
// TCR_EL1.AS is set to match in kernel/arch/arm64/mm/mmu.tkb's
// kernel_mmu_activate, because the two have to agree or the hardware reads
// a different number of bits than the software writes.
//
// **A number is reclaimed by rolling over, not by being freed.** That is
// what makes exhaustion stop being a refusal: assignment is a counter, and
// when the counter passes the last number it advances a GENERATION,
// invalidates every TLB entry, and starts again at 1. An address space
// whose recorded generation is not the current one no longer owns its
// number -- it is given a fresh one the next time it is activated
// (kernel/mm/address_space.tkb's address_space_activate), which is the one
// place a root reaches TTBR0_EL1. So asid_assign() cannot fail, and no
// process is ever refused for want of an ASID.
//
// Why there is no free list any more, and no linear AsidOwner: a freed
// ASID used to be returned to a SlotMap so it could be handed out again.
// With a generation there is nothing to return -- an abandoned number is
// reclaimed in bulk at the next rollover -- so the obligation a linear
// owner existed to enforce does not exist. What replaced "give it back" is
// "outlive the generation", and what checks it is the generation stamp
// stored beside the number.
//
// Current limitations:
// - SYNCHRONIZED, and by construction rather than by convention as of
//   GitHub issue #452. The four numbers one assignment touches live in an
//   AsidState inside a LockedCell, so no path can read or write them
//   without holding the guard -- reaching them any other way is a compile
//   error naming kernel/lib/locked_cell.tkb. Before #479 they were plain
//   globals and two cores measurably aliased 122 numbers onto two address
//   spaces each; between #479 and #452 they were locked but the binding
//   was a comment.
// - THE ONE WAY AROUND IT is asid_init/asid_assign_boot, which reach the
//   state through locked_cell_value_boot and are `!{unsafe}` so that the
//   exception is visible in their signatures and in the trusted-base
//   inventory. Both run in the MMU-off window, where a lock is impossible
//   (AArch64 has no exclusives on Device-typed memory, which is what
//   everything is until the MMU is on) and unnecessary (PSCI has not been
//   asked for a second core yet).
// - asid_bits and asid_last are still plain globals, deliberately: written
//   once by asid_init and never again, which is what keeps
//   asid_width_bits() and asid_highest_value() lock-free.
// - The ROLLOVER's reach is no longer part of that. GitHub issue #446
//   made mmu_tlb_invalidate_all() `tlbi vmalle1is`, so a rollover
//   invalidates every core in the shareability domain rather than only the
//   one that noticed the counter wrapped. A second core executing user
//   code with an ASID about to be reassigned therefore does NOT keep stale
//   entries -- the remaining single-core assumption is about the counter,
//   not about the TLB.
// - A number is not reused until the next rollover even if the address
//   space holding it dies immediately. Linux keeps a bitmap and reuses
//   freed numbers within a generation; here 65535 process creations
//   between two whole-TLB invalidations is cheap enough that the bitmap is
//   not worth its own state. What this costs is measured by
//   asid_rollover_count(), which the boot suite prints.
// - Nothing here ever invalidates by ASID on its own behalf. A rollover
//   invalidates everything; a page-table change invalidates through
//   kernel/mm/address_space.tkb, which still knows the number it recorded.

// The architectural floor: every AArch64 implementation has at least an
// 8-bit ASID field, so this is what the width query falls back to when it
// reports anything this file does not recognise.
use "kernel/lib/execution_model.tkb";
use "kernel/lib/pool_lock.tkb";
use "kernel/lib/locked_cell.tkb";

const ASID_BITS_FLOOR: usize = 8;

private let mut asid_bits: usize = ASID_BITS_FLOOR;
// The highest assignable number. ASID 0 is never assigned -- it is the
// value TTBR0_EL1 holds before any address space exists, and treating it
// as "no ASID" is what lets address_space.tkb test `asid != 0`.
private let mut asid_last: usize = 255;
private let mut asid_ready: bool = false;

// GitHub issue #452: everything one assignment touches, in one struct
// behind one lock.
//
// #479 gave these four numbers a lock and a comment saying the lock
// covered them. The compiler could not read the comment; a new read of
// asid_generation anywhere would have compiled, and this file hands out
// the number the HARDWARE compares to decide whether a TLB entry belongs
// to the address space asking. Getting it wrong is silent cross-process
// memory access with no fault to point at -- measured, before the lock,
// as 122 numbers handed to two address spaces each.
//
// They are one struct rather than four cells on purpose: a rollover
// advances the generation, resets the counter and bumps the rollover
// count as one step, and four separate cells would be four locks with an
// order to get right (issue #466). One cell, one critical section.
struct AsidState {
    // The next number to hand out. 1 after every rollover; ASID 0 is
    // never assigned, because it is what TTBR0_EL1 holds before any
    // address space exists.
    next: usize;
    // Which round of the counter this is. An address space whose recorded
    // stamp is not this value has lost its number.
    generation: usize;
    // How many times the counter has wrapped. The cost of the scheme, and
    // the boot suite prints it.
    rollover_count: usize;
    // How many numbers have been handed out, for the contention probe.
    assign_count: usize;
}

// Zeroed from .bss like every global, which is NOT the starting state
// here -- next and generation must both be 1. asid_init supplies them,
// and it runs in the MMU-off window where no lock can be taken at all
// (see asid_assign_boot below), so it goes through locked_cell_value_boot.
private let mut asid_cell: LockedCell(AsidState);

// Called from kernel_mmu_init with the MMU OFF: an mrs and some
// arithmetic, no allocation, nothing that touches a page that is not
// mapped yet (see kernel/MEMORY_MAP.md's note on that window).
fn asid_init() !{mmu_off, unsafe} {
    // No mutex_init for the cell's lock; see kernel/lib/mutex.tkb.
    if (asid_ready) { return; }
    asid_ready = true;
    asid_bits = mmu_asid_bits();
    // 1 << 16 is 65536 numbers, of which 65535 are assignable.
    asid_last = (1 << asid_bits) - 1;
    // locked_cell_value_boot, not locked_cell_value: this runs with the
    // MMU off, where spin_lock is unavailable by architecture rather than
    // by choice (no exclusives on Device-typed memory) and unnecessary
    // besides -- PSCI has not been asked for a second core yet.
    let state = locked_cell_value_boot(&asid_cell);
    state.generation = 1;
    state.next = 1;
}

fn asid_width_bits() -> usize {
    return asid_bits;
}

fn asid_highest_value() -> usize {
    return asid_last;
}

// The four readers below take the lock, which the bare globals they
// replace did not. Two reasons, and the second is the one that bit: a
// reader racing a rollover can see the new generation with the old
// counter, and a probe built on an unlocked read of the counter it is
// measuring is how the first ASID detector reported handing out more
// numbers than it asked for (issue #479).
fn asid_current_generation() -> usize {
    let guard = locked_cell_take(&asid_cell);
    let state = locked_cell_value(guard, &asid_cell);
    let generation: usize = state.generation;
    locked_cell_give(&asid_cell, guard);
    return generation;
}

fn asid_rollover_count_value() -> usize {
    let guard = locked_cell_take(&asid_cell);
    let state = locked_cell_value(guard, &asid_cell);
    let count: usize = state.rollover_count;
    locked_cell_give(&asid_cell, guard);
    return count;
}

// GitHub issue #479: the counter a contention probe compares against. Each
// successful assign advances asid_next by exactly one, so a delta smaller
// than the number of calls IS a lost race -- two cores that read the same
// value both write value+1, and one number gets handed out twice.
fn asid_next_value() -> usize {
    let guard = locked_cell_take(&asid_cell);
    let state = locked_cell_value(guard, &asid_cell);
    let next: usize = state.next;
    locked_cell_give(&asid_cell, guard);
    return next;
}

fn asid_assign_count_value() -> usize {
    let guard = locked_cell_take(&asid_cell);
    let state = locked_cell_value(guard, &asid_cell);
    let count: usize = state.assign_count;
    locked_cell_give(&asid_cell, guard);
    return count;
}

// Does this recorded stamp still name the generation the hardware's TLB is
// tagged for? A stamp of 0 is "never assigned" and is never current.
fn asid_generation_is_current(generation: usize) -> bool {
    if (generation == 0) { return false; }
    return generation == asid_current_generation();
}

// The whole cost of the scheme, in one place. Every number is about to be
// handed out again, so every entry tagged with one has to go.
private fn asid_rollover(state: borrow *AsidState) {
    state.generation = state.generation + 1;
    state.next = 1;
    state.rollover_count = state.rollover_count + 1;
    mmu_tlb_invalidate_all();
}

// (asid, generation). Cannot fail: the caller that used to have to handle
// AsidAllocResult::Full has nothing left to handle, which is the point of
// issue #402.
//
// **Call this only when switching to the address space that will hold the
// result.** A rollover hands the numbers of every live address space to
// somebody else, which is safe exactly because none of them is executing:
// the one that was is the one being switched away from, and the one that
// will be is the one taking the number this returns. Assigning at
// CREATION time instead would roll over underneath a running address
// space and alias its TLB entries with the new one's -- which is why a
// new root is created with no ASID at all and gets one at its first
// activation, the same lifetime Linux gives an mm.
// GitHub issue #479. Everything below reads and writes asid_next, and two
// cores that read it together both write value + 1 -- so one number goes to
// two address spaces, which is what the hardware compares to decide whether
// a TLB entry belongs to the address space asking. Silent cross-process
// memory access, with no fault to point at. Measured before the lock:
// 4096 assignments advanced the counter 3974 times.
//
// The lock is Mutex rather than TaskMutex for the same reason page.tkb's
// is: asid_assign is reached from address_space_activate, which pool_lock
// callers can be inside, and taking a may_block lock under an interrupt
// mask is the shape that hangs.
fn asid_assign() -> (usize, usize) {
    let guard = locked_cell_take(&asid_cell);
    let state = locked_cell_value(guard, &asid_cell);
    let result: (usize, usize) = asid_assign_locked(state);
    locked_cell_give(&asid_cell, guard);
    return result;
}

// The bootstrap entry point, for the window before the MMU is on. Not
// taking the lock is forced -- exclusives are unavailable on Device-typed
// memory, which is what spin_lock's `!{requires_mmu}` says -- and also
// correct: root 0 takes its ASID before PSCI has been asked for a second
// core.
fn asid_assign_boot() -> (usize, usize) !{mmu_off, unsafe} {
    let state = locked_cell_value_boot(&asid_cell);
    return asid_assign_locked(state);
}

// asid_last and asid_bits stay outside the cell deliberately: asid_init
// writes them once, before PSCI has been asked for a second core, and
// nothing writes them again. Keeping them out is what lets
// asid_width_bits() and asid_highest_value() stay lock-free for the
// callers that only want to print the width.
private fn asid_assign_locked(state: borrow *AsidState) -> (usize, usize) {
    if (state.next > asid_last) { asid_rollover(state); }
    let value: usize = state.next;
    state.next = state.next + 1;
    state.assign_count = state.assign_count + 1;
    return (value, state.generation);
}

// GitHub issue #479: this file HAD an execution-model assertion and no
// longer needs one, which is worth saying rather than silently deleting.
//
// It named two reasons in turn, and both are now fixed. The first was
// GitHub issue #446's -- the rollover invalidated the LOCAL TLB, so a
// second core kept entries tagged with numbers about to be reassigned.
// The second, after that landed, was that asid_next and asid_generation
// were unlocked globals and two cores in asid_assign could alias two
// address spaces onto one number. That was measured (4096 assignments
// advanced the counter 3974 times) and then locked.
//
// Everything asid_assign_locked touches -- the whole AsidState, plus
// asid_last, plus the rollover -- is inside that lock, and since GitHub
// issue #452 it is inside it structurally: the state is a private field
// of a LockedCell, so an unguarded read does not compile. The remaining
// globals (asid_bits, asid_last, asid_ready) are written once by asid_init
// before any second core exists. So there is nothing left for an assertion
// to be true ABOUT, and one that fires without a reason is worse than
// none: it sends the next reader looking for a defect that was fixed.
